CVE-2026-40226 until:2027-07-01

# systemd 257.9-1~deb13u1: udev privilege escalation via malicious hardware
# devices and unsanitized kernel output. MEDIUM. Affects libudev1.
# Attack vector requires physical access to attach a crafted hardware device
# to the host. The application is a containerised web backend deployed on
# cloud VMs (Hetzner/Fly.io); it does not use udev, does not open device
# nodes, and has no hardware enumeration code. Physical device access to
# cloud infrastructure is outside the threat model. No fix in Debian 13.
CVE-2026-40225 until:2027-07-01

# glibc 2.41-12: information disclosure or denial of service via ungetwc()
# with specific wide-character encodings. Affects libc-bin, libc6.
# Reachability analysis (2026-04-27):
#   - ungetwc() is a C-level wide-character stream API (push back a
#     wide character onto a FILE*).  Python 3 does NOT use ungetwc():
#     CPython's text I/O goes through its own codec layer
#     (Lib/_pyio.py + the io module), wrapping the underlying byte
#     stream — wide-character C functions are bypassed entirely.
#   - The only other glibc consumer in the image is Stockfish, which
#     speaks UCI text protocol over stdin/stdout: ASCII bytes only,
#     no wide-character I/O.  See llm/seca/engines/stockfish/pool.py
#     for how we feed it (the flat ``engine_pool.py`` referenced here
#     historically was retired in the engine-library cleanup).
#   - The exploit precondition (attacker-controlled wide-character
#     stream reaching ungetwc) does not occur in this image's
#     execution paths.
# Accepted risk pending a Debian glibc patch; remove this entry when
# `apt-get upgrade -y` ships a fixed libc6.
CVE-2026-5928 until:2027-07-01

# glibc 2.41-12: heap buffer overflow in scanf() with `%mc` format
# specifier and large width.  Affects libc-bin, libc6.  Confirmed via
# `trivy image` against the published API image on 2026-04-27 — same
# Trivy DB as CI, both libc-bin and libc6 entries returned no
# FixedVersion (unfixed in Debian 13 Trixie).
# Reachability analysis (2026-04-27):
#   - The vulnerable code path is the C-level scanf family with the
#     `%mc` (allocate-buffer-for-multibyte-char) modifier.  Neither
#     CPython nor any of our Python deps call scanf with %mc on
#     attacker-controlled input — Python parsing happens at the
#     interpreter level (PyArg_Parse*, struct module, etc.) and never
#     invokes scanf on byte streams from HTTP requests.
#   - Stockfish parses UCI commands with its own ad-hoc tokeniser
#     (Search/UCI.cpp), not scanf.
#   - The width-modifier overflow requires the application to pass an
#     attacker-controlled width specifier to scanf — no such code path
#     exists in this codebase.
# Accepted risk pending a Debian glibc patch; remove this entry when
# `apt-get upgrade -y` ships a fixed libc6.
CVE-2026-5450 until:2027-07-01

# util-linux 2.41-5: PAM access-control bypass via improper hostname
# canonicalization in login(1) -h <hostname>.  MEDIUM.  Affects
# bsdutils, libblkid1, liblastlog2-2, libmount1, libsmartcols1,
# libuuid1, login, mount, util-linux (9 sub-packages — all share
# the util-linux source).
# Reachability analysis (2026-05-13):
#   - The vulnerable code path is ``login(1)`` invoked with the ``-h
#     <remote_hostname>`` flag, which sets ``PAM_RHOST`` for host-based
#     PAM rules.  Exploitation requires (a) the ``login`` binary to be
#     invoked with attacker-controlled ``-h`` AND (b) PAM rules in
#     ``/etc/pam.d/*`` that key access decisions on the unsanitised
#     ``PAM_RHOST`` value.
#   - The container runs ``uvicorn llm.server:app`` as ``USER appuser``
#     (uid 10001) and never spawns ``login``.  The base image has no
#     telnetd, sshd, getty, or any other service that would invoke
#     ``login -h`` on incoming connections — only the FastAPI app
#     listens on :8000.
#   - The Dockerfile installs no ``/etc/pam.d/`` host-based rules; the
#     stock ``python:3.13-slim`` PAM config is unused at runtime
#     because no PAM-aware service is invoked.
# Accepted risk pending a Debian util-linux patch; remove this entry
# when ``apt-get upgrade -y`` ships a fixed package (Debian security
# tracker shows no fix as of 2026-05-13).
CVE-2026-3184 until:2027-07-01

# glibc 2.41-12: out-of-bounds write in ns_printrrf / ns_printrr /
# fp_nquery when printing DNS TSIG records.  MEDIUM.  Affects libc-bin
# and libc6.
# Reachability analysis (2026-05-13):
#   - The vulnerable functions are *deprecated* DNS resource-record
#     pretty-printers (``ns_printrrf``, ``ns_printrr``, ``fp_nquery``)
#     that format TSIG records for human-readable output.  Their
#     callers are DNS administration tools like ``dig`` / ``nslookup``
#     / ``host`` and DNS server software (BIND).
#   - The container runs Python (CPython resolver uses
#     ``getaddrinfo``/``gethostbyname`` — never the ``ns_print*``
#     family) and Stockfish (no networking).  Neither imports
#     ``libresolv.so.2``'s deprecated printers.
#   - The exploit precondition requires the application to call these
#     functions on attacker-controlled DNS responses — only DNS-server
#     and DNS-debugger code paths do this, and the container ships
#     neither (slim image; no ``dig``, ``nslookup``, ``bind``, etc.).
# Accepted risk pending a Debian glibc patch; remove this entry when
# ``apt-get upgrade -y`` ships a fixed libc6.
CVE-2026-5435 until:2027-07-01

# glibc 2.41-12: information disclosure / DoS in ns_printrrf /
# ns_printrr / fp_nquery — RDATA-length is not validated against
# RDATA content when formatting a DNS response, so a crafted RDATA
# field can cause a crash or read uninitialised memory.  MEDIUM.
# Affects libc-bin and libc6.  Same affected-function cluster as
# CVE-2026-5435 above (different upstream advisory, same call sites);
# the reachability analysis is identical and reproduced here so each
# CVE entry stands on its own without cross-reference churn:
# Reachability analysis (2026-05-27):
#   - The vulnerable functions are *deprecated* DNS resource-record
#     pretty-printers (``ns_printrrf``, ``ns_printrr``, ``fp_nquery``)
#     marked deprecated since glibc 2.34 — kept in libresolv only for
#     backwards compatibility with DNS administration tools and DNS
#     server software (dig, nslookup, host, BIND).  Per the Debian
#     security tracker, the functions "are for application debugging
#     only and hence not in the path of code executed by the DNS
#     resolver".
#   - The container runs Python (CPython resolver uses
#     ``getaddrinfo``/``gethostbyname`` — never the ``ns_print*``
#     family) and Stockfish (no networking).  Neither imports
#     ``libresolv.so.2``'s deprecated printers.
#   - The exploit precondition requires the application to call these
#     functions on attacker-controlled DNS responses — only DNS-server
#     and DNS-debugger code paths do this, and the container ships
#     neither (slim image; no ``dig``, ``nslookup``, ``bind``, etc.).
# Accepted risk pending a Debian glibc patch; remove this entry when
# ``apt-get upgrade -y`` ships a fixed libc6 (Debian security tracker
# shows no fix as of 2026-05-27; libc6/libc-bin still 2.41-12+deb13u3).
CVE-2026-6238 until:2027-07-01

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# perl-base 5.40.1-6 (python:3.13-slim / Debian 13 Trixie): an 11-CVE cluster,
# ALL UNFIXED in Debian (no FixedVersion via ``apt-get upgrade -y``; the
# Dockerfile already runs ``apt-get upgrade -y``).  Eight were confirmed by the
# Trivy SARIF scan on 2026-06-17 as GitHub code-scanning alerts #355-#362;
# three more (alerts #410-#412) arrived in the 2026-07-30 scan and are analysed
# in their own dated sub-block below.
#
# WHY PERL CVEs KEEP LANDING ON THIS IMAGE: Debian records perl advisories
# against the ``perl`` SOURCE package, and Trivy maps a source-package advisory
# onto every binary package built from it that is present in the image.  The
# only perl binary package in ``python:3.13-slim`` is ``perl-base``, so every
# perl CVE is reported against it — whether or not the affected code actually
# ships inside that binary package.  It frequently does not: perl-base is the
# minimal Essential subset, and most flagged modules live in
# ``perl-modules-5.40`` / ``libperl5.40``, neither of which is installed here.
# See the CVE-2026-57433 (Storable) entry below for a worked example.
#
# ``perl-base`` is a Debian ``Essential: yes`` / ``Priority: required``
# package: it ships in every Debian base image to support dpkg/apt maintainer
# scripts and cannot be removed without breaking the package manager.  It is
# NOT an application dependency.
#
# Reachability analysis (2026-06-17) — shared by all 8 entries below:
#   - The runtime image launches ``uvicorn llm.server:app`` as USER appuser
#     (uid 10001).  The app is pure CPython; the HEALTHCHECK is python; the
#     ONLY external binary the code spawns is Stockfish, located via
#     ``shutil.which("stockfish")`` (llm/server.py:328) and driven over the
#     UCI text protocol (ASCII bytes only — see
#     llm/seca/engines/stockfish/pool.py).
#   - The perl INTERPRETER is never executed: no ``system("perl")``, no
#     subprocess spawning perl, no CGI, no ``.pl``/``.cgi`` handlers, and no
#     first-party reference to perl anywhere in ``llm/`` (grep is clean).
#   - Every vulnerable code path requires perl to run on attacker-influenced
#     input: regex/pattern compilation (CVE-2026-8376) and the Archive::Tar /
#     IO::Compress / IO::Uncompress / HTTP::Tiny modules (the other seven).
#     Those modules are not part of the minimal perl-base, and nothing in the
#     image invokes them.
# Accepted risk pending a Debian perl patch; remove each entry once
# ``apt-get upgrade -y`` ships a fixed perl-base.

# CVE-2026-8376 CRITICAL: heap buffer overflow compiling a crafted pattern (alert #356)
CVE-2026-8376 until:2027-07-01
# CVE-2026-42496 CRITICAL: Archive::Tar path traversal via crafted symlinks (alert #355)
CVE-2026-42496 until:2027-07-01
# CVE-2026-42497 HIGH: Archive::Tar extracts hardlinks to attacker paths (alert #357)
CVE-2026-42497 until:2027-07-01
# CVE-2026-48959 HIGH: IO::Uncompress::Unzip CPU exhaustion (alert #358)
CVE-2026-48959 until:2027-07-01
# CVE-2026-48962 HIGH: IO::Compress arbitrary code execution via output glob (alert #359)
CVE-2026-48962 until:2027-07-01
# CVE-2026-9538 HIGH: Archive::Tar memory exhaustion (alert #360)
CVE-2026-9538 until:2027-07-01
# CVE-2025-15649 MEDIUM: IO::Uncompress::Unzip propagates uncaught error (alert #361)
CVE-2025-15649 until:2027-07-01
# CVE-2026-7010 MEDIUM: HTTP::Tiny does not validate CRLF in request fields (alert #362)
CVE-2026-7010 until:2027-07-01

# --- 2026-07-30 scan additions: alerts #410-#412 ---------------------------
# Three further perl source-package CVEs against the same perl-base 5.40.1-6.
# No-fix status re-verified directly against the Debian security tracker on
# 2026-08-27 (not merely taken from the Trivy DB snapshot): all three list
# trixie as "vulnerable" at 5.40.1-6 and are fixed only in sid/forky 5.42.3-1
# (upstream perl v5.43.10 / v5.43.11).  There is no trixie backport, so the
# ``apt-get upgrade -y`` already in llm/Dockerfile.api cannot clear them and
# the BUILDDATE cache-bust rebuild will keep re-reporting them.
#
# The shared reachability analysis above applies unchanged, re-verified
# 2026-08-27: the perl interpreter is never executed in this image.  The only
# external process the runtime spawns is Stockfish, and ``grep -rin "perl"``
# over ``llm/`` returns no first-party hit.  Per-CVE detail:
#
# CVE-2026-57433 CRITICAL (alert #411): signed integer overflow in Storable's
#   ``retrieve_hook_common`` — an SX_HOOK record carries a signed 32-bit item
#   count, and a count of I32_MAX wraps the ``av_extend(count + 1)`` addition
#   negative.  The trigger is ``Storable::thaw``/``retrieve`` on a crafted
#   blob, and the advisory's own impact statement is that ``av_extend``
#   receives the negative count and "dies with a panic, terminating the
#   deserialization" — a DoS of the deserializing perl process, not code
#   execution, despite the CRITICAL label.
#   Unreachable here for TWO independent reasons.  (1) Storable is not merely
#   unused, it is ABSENT: it is an XS module shipped in ``perl-modules-5.40`` /
#   ``libperl5.40``, and the trixie ``perl-base`` file list contains no
#   ``Storable.pm`` / ``Storable.so``.  Neither package is installed in
#   ``python:3.13-slim``, so the vulnerable code is not in the image at all —
#   this alert is purely the source-package mapping described in the cluster
#   header.  (2) Even had it shipped, nothing would call it: there is no perl
#   process, no Storable blob, and no deserialization of one.
# CVE-2026-57432 HIGH (alert #412): integer overflow in ``S_measure_struct``
#   (pp_pack.c) — an unchecked repeat count in a ``pack``/``unpack`` template
#   wraps the signed total negative, advancing the buffer pointer out of bounds
#   and exposing heap memory to the caller.  Unlike Storable, ``pack`` and
#   ``unpack`` are interpreter builtins, so this code IS present inside
#   ``perl-base``'s ``/usr/bin/perl``.  Reachability therefore rests on the
#   single load-bearing fact: reaching it needs a perl program running an
#   attacker-influenced template, and no perl program runs here.
# CVE-2026-13221 CRITICAL (alert #410): a regex alternation with more than
#   65,535 fixed-string branches overflows the 16-bit delta field of the
#   compiled trie's match table, silently truncating it and yielding both
#   false-positive and false-negative matches.  The harm is confined to code
#   that lets perl regexes make access-control or filtering decisions.  Same
#   interpreter-core location and same class as the already-accepted
#   CVE-2026-8376 above, and the same conclusion: this image delegates no
#   security decision to a perl regex, because it runs no perl.
#
# NOTE: none of the three ever reached the CRITICAL deploy gate in
# .github/workflows/fly-deploy.yml — that step scans with
# ``ignore-unfixed: true`` and these carry no FixedVersion.  They appear only
# in the SARIF step (``ignore-unfixed: false``), which deliberately reports
# unfixed findings to the Security tab for triage.  Listing them here retires
# them from that triage queue with a written rationale; it does not weaken any
# gate.
# Accepted risk pending a Debian perl patch; remove each entry once
# ``apt-get upgrade -y`` ships a fixed perl-base — re-check the trixie row at
# https://security-tracker.debian.org/tracker/source-package/perl.
CVE-2026-57433 until:2027-07-01
CVE-2026-57432 until:2027-07-01
CVE-2026-13221 until:2027-07-01

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# libsqlite3-0 3.46.1-7+deb13u1 (python:3.13-slim / Debian 13 Trixie): two
# FTS5 full-text-search memory-corruption CVEs, BOTH UNFIXED in Debian (no
# FixedVersion via ``apt-get upgrade -y``).  Surfaced by the Trivy SARIF scan
# on 2026-06-17 as GitHub code-scanning alerts #353 (CVE-2026-11822, HIGH —
# OOB read in fts5LeafSeek + heap write) and #354 (CVE-2026-11824, HIGH —
# heap buffer overflow in fts5ChunkIterate).
#
# Reachability analysis (2026-06-17) — shared by both entries below:
#   - Both CVEs require executing an FTS5 ``MATCH`` query against an
#     ATTACKER-CONTROLLED SQLite database (crafted FTS5 leaf / continuation
#     pages).  Exploitation is impossible without opening a malicious .sqlite
#     file AND running a full-text-search query against it.
#   - Production uses PostgreSQL (psycopg2 → Hetzner Postgres), not SQLite.
#     SQLite appears only on the dev/test path via SQLAlchemy over an
#     app-created schema; the service never opens an externally-supplied
#     database file.
#   - The codebase uses NO FTS5 extension and issues NO ``MATCH`` queries:
#     ``grep -rin "fts5\|MATCH\|enable_load_extension" llm/`` returns only
#     Python ``re.match`` hits.  The vulnerable FTS5 code path is never
#     exercised on any input this service handles.
# Accepted risk pending a Debian sqlite3 patch; remove both entries once
# ``apt-get upgrade -y`` ships a fixed libsqlite3-0.
CVE-2026-11822 until:2027-07-01
CVE-2026-11824 until:2027-07-01

# libbz2-1.0 1.0.8-6 (python:3.13-slim / Debian 13 Trixie): off-by-one
# out-of-bounds write in the ``bzip2recover`` UTILITY → memory corruption /
# crash (DoS).  MEDIUM.  UNFIXED in Debian (no FixedVersion).  Surfaced by
# the Trivy SARIF scan on 2026-06-17 as GitHub code-scanning alert #352
# (CVE-2026-42250).
# Reachability analysis (2026-06-17):
#   - The flaw lives in the standalone ``bzip2recover`` command-line tool,
#     which reconstructs damaged ``.bz2`` archives.  The runtime image never
#     invokes it: no subprocess spawns ``bzip2recover``, there is no .bz2
#     recovery path, and the service processes no ``.bz2`` archives at all
#     (``grep -rin "bz2\|bzip2recover" llm/`` is clean).
#   - Python's stdlib ``bz2`` module exposes the libbz2 compression /
#     decompression routines, NOT the separate ``bzip2recover`` program where
#     this bug lives; even a bz2-using app would not reach the vulnerable
#     code.
# Accepted risk pending a Debian bzip2 patch; remove this entry once
# ``apt-get upgrade -y`` ships a fixed libbz2-1.0.
CVE-2026-42250 until:2027-07-01

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2026-08-27 scan batch: alerts #408-#409, #416-#419, #421-#424, #427-#428.
# The first image rebuild in three weeks (fresher Trivy DB plus newer Debian
# package versions) surfaced eight further CVEs across four source packages.
# ALL EIGHT are confirmed UNFIXED in Debian 13 Trixie and every one carries
# the tracker note ``<no-dsa> (Minor issue)`` — Debian has explicitly decided
# not to issue a security advisory for them in stable, so no amount of
# ``apt-get upgrade -y`` will clear them.  Each was checked individually
# against its per-CVE page on the Debian security tracker on 2026-08-27
# rather than trusting the Trivy DB snapshot; unlike CVE-2026-14456 in the
# BACKPORT FALSE POSITIVES section below, none of these is already fixed.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# glibc 2.41-12+deb13u3: two flaws in the ``wordexp(3)`` family.
#   CVE-2026-6368 (alerts #416 libc-bin / #418 libc6): wordexp() called with
#     WRDE_APPEND can leave invalid memory in the ``we_wordv`` member, so a
#     subsequent wordfree() aborts the process.
#   CVE-2026-6791 (alerts #417 libc-bin / #419 libc6): parse_tilde() does not
#     bounds-check the username it copies onto the stack, so a username of
#     thousands of characters exhausts the thread stack (stack clash).
# Reachability analysis (2026-08-27) — shared by both:
#   - Both live in wordexp(), the POSIX shell-style word-expansion API, and
#     nothing in this image calls it.  The Python standard library exposes no
#     wordexp binding at all; ``os.path.expanduser`` and ``glob`` are
#     implemented in pure Python (Lib/posixpath.py, Lib/glob.py) and
#     ``subprocess`` goes straight to fork/execve.  Shells do their own word
#     expansion rather than calling glibc's.
#   - No code path in the image runs a shell: ``grep -rin
#     "wordexp\|os.system\|os.popen\|expanduser\|expandvars"`` over ``llm/``
#     is clean, there is no ``shell=True`` anywhere, and the only external
#     process the runtime spawns is Stockfish, located by
#     ``shutil.which("stockfish")`` (llm/server.py:328) and driven over the
#     UCI text protocol.
#   - CVE-2026-6791 additionally needs an attacker-controlled USERNAME to
#     reach parse_tilde.  The service expands no ``~user`` string at all, let
#     alone one taken from a request.
# Accepted risk pending a Debian glibc patch (fixed upstream in 2.43-3;
# trixie marked <no-dsa>).  Remove once apt-get upgrade -y ships a fix.
CVE-2026-6368 until:2027-07-01
CVE-2026-6791 until:2027-07-01

# systemd 257.13-1~deb13u1: two local flaws, both in systemd DAEMONS rather
# than in the libraries this image links.
#   CVE-2026-15059 (alerts #421 libsystemd0 / #423 libudev1): missing path
#     traversal validation in the systemd-oomd IPC API lets a local
#     unprivileged user terminate arbitrary local processes.
#   CVE-2026-16742 (alerts #422 libsystemd0 / #424 libudev1): systemd-homed
#     privilege escalation by adding a logged-in homed-managed user to an
#     arbitrary system group.
# Reachability analysis (2026-08-27) — shared by both, and the same shape as
# the systemd entries above (CVE-2026-29111 / -4105 / -40226 / -40225):
#   - The image contains the shared LIBRARIES ``libsystemd0`` and
#     ``libudev1`` only, pulled in transitively.  It ships no systemd init
#     system, no systemd-oomd, no systemd-homed and no D-Bus system bus —
#     and the vulnerable code lives in exactly those daemons, not in the
#     libraries we link against.
#   - The container runs one process, ``uvicorn llm.server:app``, as USER
#     appuser (uid 10001).  There is no oomd socket to address, no
#     homed-managed user to promote, and no second local unprivileged
#     session to escalate from; both CVEs presuppose a local multi-user
#     system that a single-process container is not.
#   - ``grep -rin "systemd\|udev\|dbus\|oomd\|homed"`` over ``llm/`` returns
#     one comment in a CI test about the Android emulator's /dev/kvm udev
#     rule.  Nothing in the runtime touches any of it.
# Accepted risk; trixie marked <no-dsa> (Minor issue).
CVE-2026-15059 until:2027-07-01
CVE-2026-16742 until:2027-07-01

# tar 1.35+dfsg-3.1: two archive-extraction boundary escapes.
#   CVE-2026-18477 (alert #427): TOCTOU in incremental dumpdir 'X' rename
#     handling — a local attacker with write access to a directory can
#     influence a RESTORE so that files are created, renamed or overwritten
#     outside the intended extraction directory.
#   CVE-2026-18508 (alert #428): under ``--one-top-level``, a crafted archive
#     can create hardlinks that escape the boundary and, combined with a
#     preexisting symlink under the working directory, write outside it.
# Reachability analysis (2026-08-27) — shared by both.  Sibling of
# CVE-2026-5704 above (same package), but argued independently here:
#   - Both require RUNNING GNU tar to extract an attacker-supplied archive.
#     The runtime image never invokes tar — CMD is uvicorn, the HEALTHCHECK
#     is python, and the only external binary spawned is Stockfish.  tar is
#     present solely because the base image ships it for apt's use at build
#     time.
#   - The service extracts no archives at all: ``grep -rin
#     "tarfile\|unpack_archive\|make_archive\|zipfile"`` over ``llm/`` is
#     clean.  Note further that Python's ``tarfile`` module is a pure-Python
#     implementation — even a future caller would not execute GNU tar's
#     code, so these two CVEs would still not apply.
#   - CVE-2026-18477 additionally requires a local attacker holding write
#     access to the target directory AND an incremental restore in progress.
#     The container runs a single process as one non-root user, performs no
#     restores, and its rootfs is read-only apart from the /app/data volume
#     and a /tmp tmpfs (docker-compose.prod.yml, pinned by CH_03 / CH_04 in
#     llm/tests/test_container_hardening.py).
# Accepted risk; trixie marked <no-dsa> (Minor issue).
CVE-2026-18477 until:2027-07-01
CVE-2026-18508 until:2027-07-01

# libsqlite3-0 3.46.1-7+deb13u1: two flaws in the SQLite SESSION EXTENSION.
#   CVE-2026-50812 (alert #408): NULL pointer dereference — a malformed
#     changeset blob passed to sqlite3changeset_apply_v3() reaches
#     sqlite3_value_type() with a NULL pointer and crashes the process (DoS).
#   CVE-2026-50813 (alert #409): information disclosure through the changeset
#     concat / changegroup merge path.
# Reachability analysis (2026-08-27) — shared by both.  This is a DIFFERENT
# code path from the FTS5 pair above (CVE-2026-11822 / -11824), so the case
# is made independently rather than by cross-reference:
#   - Both live in SQLite's optional Session Extension (the
#     ``sqlite3session.h`` / ``sqlite3changeset_*`` API).  CPython's
#     ``sqlite3`` module does not wrap that API at all — no changeset,
#     changegroup or session object is exposed to Python — so no Python code
#     in this image can reach the vulnerable functions.
#   - Exploitation additionally requires feeding an attacker-controlled
#     CHANGESET BLOB to the extension.  The service produces and consumes no
#     changesets: ``grep -rin
#     "changeset\|sqlite3session\|load_extension"`` over ``llm/`` is clean.
#   - Production runs PostgreSQL (psycopg2 -> Hetzner Postgres).  SQLite
#     appears only on the dev/test path via SQLAlchemy over an app-created
#     schema, and the service never opens an externally-supplied database
#     file.
# Accepted risk pending a Debian sqlite3 patch (fixed in sid/forky 3.53.4-2;
# trixie marked <no-dsa>).
CVE-2026-50812 until:2027-07-01
CVE-2026-50813 until:2027-07-01

# ===========================================================================
# BACKPORT FALSE POSITIVES — NOT accepted risk.
#
# Everything above this banner is UNFIXED in Debian.  Entries below are the
# opposite case: the CVE IS fixed in the package version the image installs,
# and the scanner is simply wrong about it.  Deliberately SHORT expiries —
# these are meant to be deleted once the scanner's data catches up, never
# renewed.  A renewal request is a signal to re-verify the premise instead.
# ===========================================================================

# openssl 3.5.7-1~deb13u2 (python:3.13-slim / Debian 13 Trixie).  Reported
# against three binary packages of the ``openssl`` source package as GitHub
# code-scanning alerts #420 (libssl3t64), #425 (openssl) and #426
# (openssl-provider-legacy), all HIGH, all CVE-2026-14456 — "Unbounded Memory
# Growth in QUIC Server Incoming Channel Queue": a QUIC listener queues an
# unbounded number of incoming channels for unknown destination connection
# IDs, so a peer sending Initial packets faster than the application accepts
# connections exhausts memory (DoS).
#
# THE IMAGE IS ALREADY PATCHED.  Debian DSA-6465-1 (2026-08-25) states: "For
# the stable distribution (trixie), these problems have been fixed in version
# 3.5.7-1~deb13u2" — which is EXACTLY the version Trivy itself reports as
# installed.  The advisory landed two days before the image picked it up via
# the ``apt-get upgrade -y`` in llm/Dockerfile.api.
#
# Why it is still reported: upstream OpenSSL fixed this in 3.5.8, and Debian
# backported the patch into 3.5.7-1~deb13u2 rather than bumping the version.
# A scanner matching on the UPSTREAM fix version sees 3.5.7 < 3.5.8 and calls
# it vulnerable.  The tell for this class is the empty ``Fixed Version`` field
# in the finding: Trivy has no Debian fixed-version mapping for it, so it is
# not using Debian's advisory data.  Verified against
# https://security-tracker.debian.org/tracker/CVE-2026-14456 on 2026-08-27.
#
# There is consequently NOTHING to upgrade to: trixie ships no openssl newer
# than 3.5.7-1~deb13u2, and upstream 3.5.8 is not packaged for it.
#
# Secondary, independent of the patch: the vulnerable code path is the QUIC
# SERVER listener (``SSL_LISTENER``), which this image never instantiates.
# The container runs ``uvicorn llm.server:app`` speaking HTTP/1.1 on port
# 8000; TLS is terminated by Caddy in a separate container using Go's
# quic-go, not OpenSSL.  Python's ``ssl`` module exposes no QUIC server API
# at all, and the image's only OpenSSL use is as a TLS *client* (outbound
# HTTPS to the DeepSeek and Lichess APIs).
#
# REMOVE THIS ENTRY once the scan stops reporting CVE-2026-14456 — it should
# expire, not be renewed.  If it is still firing at the expiry date, re-check
# that the installed version is >= 3.5.7-1~deb13u2 BEFORE extending it.
CVE-2026-14456 until:2026-11-01
